I started playing NH a couple of weeks ago. Normally, ADOM is my
roguelike of choice, - just a matter of personal preference, - but
after scoring five victories there in 2004 I was getting a little
tired of it. I looked for other roguelikes on my HDD, played some
ancient build of vanilla Angband a little, and then discovered
NH3.4.0 for Windows somewhere deep in my archives.

I played mostly Priests, Samurais and Wizards with some Healers,
Rangers and Archeologists thrown in for a good measure. All with
notably little luck. I started thinking that I was, like, totally
hopeless, or maybe that NH was a devilishly hard game, much worse
in that respect than my beloved ADOM, and then, one of my chars got
insanely lucky.

He's found WoW (0:1) early on - my first ever - became my first
character to see the bottom of the mines, then, just above the
Quest Portal, he's found another WoW - that one was (0:3), and
just after draining that he managed to pull a wish off some smoky
potions, for a total of 13 wishes. That's 6.5 times more than all
of my previous characters combined have ever seen. The guy had
everything - SDSM, CoMR, speed boots, gauntlets of power, an AoLS,
rings galore, Frost Brand and Grayswandir, a magic marker or two
and AC:-25. He has genocided 38 species.

Then he entered the Quest and on the bottom level discovered in
shock that he lacked just one thing - a wand of death.

So Ashikaga Takauji bisected him. Twice.

I almost banged my head against the wall realizing that I just
missed my best ever chance at ascension - that I wouldn't get
another any time soon. So I was back to my misery, dying to dwarves
in mines and such.

On the next day I tried several other roles, and, strangely enough,
one of those characters managed to become a demigod.

Unlike the aforementioned uber-lucky Samurai, this Valkyrie didn't
seem to be lucky at all, at all. She made her way through the early
game with relative ease, pet-robbed a couple of shops on Dlvl2 and
3, got to Minetown, picked up a banded mail and found a BoH
somewhere, then another one in Sokoban.

Converted the starting long sword into Excalibur on the Oracle
level (almost died to water mocassins in the process). Raided the
vaults and bought some 3 to 4 points of protection.

Got to the bottom of the mines, finding a nice cloak of protection
somewhere, got the booze and the luckstone from the Gnome King's
Wine Cellar, then went deeper into whatever-the-main-dungeon-is-
called-in-NetHack :-) On Dlvl9 Sophia found her first dungeon
altar.

A few corpses later she walked away with Mjollnir in her hands.

Her armor at this point consisted of a dwarven mithril coat, some
dwarven helm and boots, CoP, and the starting +3 small shield.

Around Dlvl11 she's found a wand of death, and did the Quest soon
after. Was very worried about those fire traps on ice and those
drawbridges, but it all went well. Excalibur took care of traps,
wands of striking took care of drawbridges, wand of death took care
of Lord Surtur, and tinning kit took care of those corpses.

The only problem with the quest was one of the giants firing some
wand, destroying Sophia's only wand of cancellation. She's never
found another one, and had to resort to some fancy dipping to get
enough water.

Around the Rogue level Sophia has encountered a polymorph trap, and
was happy to discover that one of her rings was polymorph control.
A change into another Valkyrie wasn't all bad. The remaining tins
of giants and some gelee royale helped strength, and in the Xp/HP
department it was actually a gain. Unfortunately, Sophia's trusty
large dog, Fido, stepped into that trap, too, and was polymorphed
into a shark. Sophia didn't have any wands or potions of polymorph
(she hadn't seen those in the entire game), and some passer-by took
that pathetic 1HP off Fido before she could lure him back into the
trap.

Polymorph traps continued to be an annoyance for a long long time,
but the last change, while clearing the Castle, was also the worst
one. Not only it was a huge loss in stats, but Sophia also was
shocked to find a penis between her legs. Soon after that Sophia's
got her, um, his GDSM, fixed stats with alchemy and didn't want to
risk any more polymorphs, so s/he had to stay in that highly
unnatural form.

OOC: Damn, I really think Valkyries should be immune to those
gender changes. It just feels WRONG, horribly WRONG.

On Dlvl16 there was a temple, which eventually got Sophia's naked
AC to 0.

Coaligned altars were on Dlvl23 and 25, and the latter one became
her base of operations. Search for Fort Ludios was in vain, and
Medusa was scary without genociding those ;s (a couple of genocide
scrolls Sophia has found went on Ls and hs). So she just wandered
around, sacrificing some monsters, getting Fire Brand, Grayswandir
and Dragonbane, but it didn't help at all. In the end, she decided
to risk those eels, and they really weren't very tough, even while
wearing -3 boots of levitation. The only thing in Perseus' statue
was another pair of those boots. Well, at least those were at +0...

The Castle was quite a walk in the park either, save for a couple
of nasty disenchanters and the aforementioned Gender Bender (R)
Polymorph Trap (tm).

Unfortunately, she, er, he was all too eager to get his hands on that
WoW (it turned out to be 0:1), and didn't check the store
rooms before his wishing session. After wishing for 2 blessed
scrolls of charging, GDSM, shield of reflection, speed boots and
gauntlets of power it was quite an outrage for him to find
the latter two just a dozen a steps away. There also was a Hawaiian
shirt there and a pre-blessed magic lamp. Sophia really should've
saved that for a light source, since he didn't get a wish out of it
anyway, hitting that 5% chance of a hostile djinni instead.

(Btw, some monster had thrown a smoky potion - it was sickness in
this game - at Sophia in the early game, and a djinni was released,
muttering "It's about time..." I wonder who would've gotten a wish
if that potion would've been blessed and the RNG rolled that 4/5
chance?)

Ever since entering Gehennom Sophia has wielded the Excalibur, both
for it searching abilities and level-drain resistance, only
switching to Grayswandir for demon lords. Juiblex was trivial, both
Asmodeus and Baalzebub did some running around, but fell to
Sophia's silver saber eventually, and Orcus didn't even escape to
another level, and only spent one charge of his WoDeath. Also,
some stupid marilith summoned Yeenoghu on the first turn of
combat, but he wasn't too much of a trouble, either, although he
managed to escape something like three times.

Sophia didn't explore the mazes fully, concentrating on making
paths between the stairs. The only notable find in Gehennom was
the second magic marker in the game, which, among other things,
allowed Sophia a couple more genocides and a humble nurse orgy in
Sokoban.

(It was a mess-up, actually, - I didn't realize markers could only
be recharged once, so I missed on scrolls of remove curse, which
made me very very nervous in the endgame.)

Having disposed of Vlad, Sophia has found the VS on Dlvl50, cleared
the first two levels of Rodney's tower, checked the path back to
surface, made all the final preparations necessary, and faced the
Wizard. Perhaps the only problem was Sophia's lack of curse
removal - just one scroll, and a heap of useless-potions-turned-
holy-water in his sack.

Rodney died to a single death ray. Level teleport to Dlvl50 ->
invocation -> Moloch's Sanctum. The fight was a little hard, but at
least the high priest himself died to a single death ray, too.
After that, a really quick run to the surface: all those scrolls
and wands of teleportation were finally of use. (So all that
diggin' between the stairs was NOT of use...) The mysterious force
didn't bother Sophia too much, and the Wizard was pretty gentle,
too: a couple of malignant auras and a couple of nasties summons.

On the Earth Plane Wizard died to a single death ray again. The rest of
the plane was easy.

Air was rather hellish, and Sophia escaped it with barely a hundred
HPs.

Fire, easy but annoying.

Water took those last two charges of the Orb of Fate, but was
otherwise quite easy, too.

On the Astral Plane Sophia decided to take on Pestilence first - it
was on the left. Wands of death in. Neutral altar. Wands of death
out.

Famine was in the center. Wands of death in, the altar's chaotic,
wands of death out.

Sophia was quite afraid of Death, but the freak fell to the
Excalibur quickly, the altar wasn't hard to get to, and soon, very
soon, the mighty male Valkyrie became the minor deity of all things
transsexual... duh.

